theory.intro:
The number plane has two axes that cross at the origin $(0,0)$, dividing it into four quadrants. Each point is given by an ordered pair $(x, y)$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.62]
\draw[gray!30,very thin] (-5,-5) grid (5,5);
\draw[<->] (-5.5,0)--(5.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[<->] (0,-5.5)--(0,5.5) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \n in {-5,-4,-3,-2,-1,1,2,3,4,5}{\node[below,font=\small] at (\n,0) {$\n$};\node[left,font=\small] at (0,\n) {$\n$};}
\node[below left,font=\small] at (0,0) {$0$};
\node[font=\small] at (2.7,2.7) {$x>0,\ y>0$};
\node[font=\small] at (-2.7,2.7) {$x<0,\ y>0$};
\node[font=\small] at (-2.7,-2.7) {$x<0,\ y<0$};
\node[font=\small] at (2.7,-2.7) {$x>0,\ y<0$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

